WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.109
In this walkthrough we do Lab 3 in Rosetta DBT Studio.

00:00:07.389 --> 00:00:13.746
We declare the raw tables as sources, build three staging models and two marts, and run them on DuckDB.

00:00:14.696 --> 00:00:24.507
You need the end of Lab 1: the raw customers, orders and payments tables in your DuckDB file, and the dbt course project with an empty models folder.

00:00:25.457 --> 00:00:27.603
Open the dbt course project.

00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:29.630
The models folder is still empty.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.728
Right-click models, choose New Folder, and name it staging.

00:00:34.008 --> 00:00:36.071
Then make a second folder, called marts.

00:00:41.488 --> 00:00:43.603
In staging, create the sources file.

00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:47.628
Its name starts with an underscore, so it stays at the top of the folder.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:54.904
It declares one source called raw, in the schema raw, with three tables: customers, orders and payments.

00:00:55.854 --> 00:00:56.744
Save the file.

00:00:57.024 --> 00:01:04.149
From now on, models read raw data through source, and dbt can trace lineage all the way back to these tables.

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:09.551
Next, one staging model per source table, starting with staging customers.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.088
Light cleanup only.

00:01:13.368 --> 00:01:17.278
Rename id to customer id, and cast created at to a date.

00:01:17.558 --> 00:01:19.919
No joins, and no business logic.

00:01:20.199 --> 00:01:20.816
Save it.

00:01:21.766 --> 00:01:27.202
Staging orders follows the same pattern: rename the id, cast the order date, and save.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:38.023
Staging payments renames the method, and turns cents into dollars.

00:01:38.303 --> 00:01:39.406
Save that one too.

00:01:47.044 --> 00:01:47.983
Now the marts.

00:01:48.263 --> 00:01:50.520
In the marts folder, create fact orders.

00:01:53.624 --> 00:01:55.781
It is the model from Lesson 3.2.

00:01:56.061 --> 00:01:58.450
First the orders, from staging, through ref.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.360
Then the payments, added up per order.

00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:05.807
And one row per order, with its amount.

00:02:06.087 --> 00:02:07.028
Save the model.

00:02:10.774 --> 00:02:13.547
Next, dim customers: one row per customer.

00:02:17.568 --> 00:02:23.335
It keeps the customer columns, and adds the first order date, the number of orders, and the lifetime value.

00:02:25.068 --> 00:02:28.318
Those come from fact orders, joined on customer id.

00:02:28.598 --> 00:02:32.473
Because of this ref, dbt will always build fact orders first.

00:02:32.753 --> 00:02:33.370
Save it.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.397
Open dbt project dot yml, and go to the end of the file.

00:02:39.347 --> 00:02:40.519
Add a models block.

00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:45.237
Everything in staging becomes a view, and everything in marts becomes a table.

00:02:46.187 --> 00:02:46.804
Save it.

00:02:47.084 --> 00:02:50.856
These are folder defaults, and a single model can still override them.

00:02:51.806 --> 00:02:54.559
Now open the Project menu, and choose Compile.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.381
In target, compiled, open fact orders.

00:02:59.661 --> 00:03:03.466
Every ref has become a real table name in your DuckDB file.

00:03:04.416 --> 00:03:06.420
Then the Project menu again, and Run.

00:03:09.494 --> 00:03:13.633
Five models, built in dependency order: three views and two tables.

00:03:13.913 --> 00:03:16.272
The last line says pass five, error zero.

00:03:17.222 --> 00:03:18.385
Let's check the data.

00:03:18.665 --> 00:03:25.238
Open the SQL Editor, pick the course connection, and ask for each customer's order count and lifetime value.

00:03:29.774 --> 00:03:32.044
Run it with the green arrow next to the line.

00:03:33.951 --> 00:03:37.605
Ava comes first, with three orders and one hundred and two dollars.

00:03:37.885 --> 00:03:38.885
Farid has none.

00:03:39.165 --> 00:03:42.305
That is exactly the expected result on the lab page.

00:03:43.255 --> 00:03:44.364
Last, the lineage.

00:03:44.644 --> 00:03:48.299
Back in the project, open dim customers, and the Lineage tab.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.664
Maximize it, and set the depth to three.

00:04:03.681 --> 00:04:10.958
That is the whole lab in one picture: the raw sources on the left, the staging views in the middle, and the two marts on the right.

00:04:11.908 --> 00:04:12.977
That is Lab 3.

00:04:13.257 --> 00:04:20.361
The raw data stayed untouched, the staging layer cleaned it once, and the marts were built in the right order by one command.

00:04:21.311 --> 00:04:25.174
Before you move on, answer the reflection questions on the lab page.

00:04:25.454 --> 00:04:31.865
Try changing staging orders, and run it with a plus sign after the name, to rebuild everything downstream.
